s a plausible route to recover those failures without changing the official task or saved-policy semantics.", "observations_sought": "Measure the transfer-trained checkpoint trajectory on the unchanged development task, including overall success, failures by target radius and angle, and whether the persistent failure seeds improve without introducing failures outside the focused sector.", "uncertainty": "The persistent sector may reflect insufficient exposure to near targets and negative angles, but the same pattern could arise from a learned representation or control limitation; the single baseline seed cannot distinguish these explanations."}, "scientific_question": "Can broader target-coverage training with extra exposure to the persistent negative-angle sector reduce the residual failures without sacrificing success elsewhere?", "training_parent": "working"}, "reasoning": {"clarification": "Improvement concentrated in the targeted sector would make training coverage a stronger practical explanation, while unchanged sector failures or new failures elsewhere would indicate that coverage alone is insufficient and would expose the tradeoff. Either result remains exploratory evidence rather than causal proof.", "evidence": [{"observation": "The campaign objective is at least 98% success on the unchanged official task, whose targets span radii 6-20 cm and the full angular range.", "source": "research/scenario.md"}, {"observation": "Checkpoint-100352 reached 97.0% (194/200); six failures had angles from -122.1 to -145.4 degrees, with five never entering tolerance.", "source": "research/evaluations/045ec01f-613e-4cd7-9cac-4b3c512b0f94/evaluation-045ec01f-613e-4cd7-9cac-4b3c512b0f94-experiment-1-checkpoint-100352-200ep-seed0-6ba3ba6d7654.json"}, {"observation": "Checkpoint-120832 retained the same six negative-angle failures, added a distinct failure after brief tolerance entry, and scored 96.5% (193/200).", "source": "research/evaluations/045ec01f-613e-4cd7-9cac-4b3c512